WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.211
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.211 --> 00:00:09.390
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.970
&gt;&gt; We have eight minutes
to ask a question..

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:16.150
And we're gonna get there
through tangents, and

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.150
we're gonna get there through metaphors.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.600
And we're gonna do it that way because
this guy is a diversion thinker.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.070
And that's a term we're gonna get to
a little bit later on in this talk.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.900
But for now,
the question what do we take when we exit?

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.415
This is the road out of this place.

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:36.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's the view from

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.870
the main quad down toward Palo Alto.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.480
And here at the business school,
we have classes about exit strategies.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.540
These classes teach us how to take
an idea, turn it into a product and

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.050
exit with that product into a marketplace.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.040
The names for these classes From Idea
to Launch, From Launch to Liquidity.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.750
Dean Saloner is quoting Buzz Lightyear for

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.615
next year's seminar,
To Liquidity and Beyond.

00:01:02.615 --> 00:01:05.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.840
&gt;&gt; Now in order to provide some
context for this question,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.260
I'm going to talk about
an exit that I had in my life.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.050
It was not a liquidity event.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.530
It was the end of my dance career.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.880
So, we're going to step through time.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.460
I'm 21 years old.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.450
I have no college education, and

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.280
I'm standing in front of a man who
will be nominated for a Tony Award for

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.750
The Color Purple, and he has just
given me this phrase of arm movement.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.334
And he says Aaron,
put that movement into your legs.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:48.630
&gt;&gt; And I said Donald, in my head, no!

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.660
Arms are not legs!

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.140
They do not have the same weights.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.230
They do not have the same joints.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.490
They will not move the same way.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:58.660
This is crazy.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.115
Outside I go with him on this tangent and
I say, okay.

00:02:03.115 --> 00:02:07.169
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I move my leg around,

00:02:07.169 --> 00:02:10.010
I step up to it, and I jump back.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.200
As literal of an interpretation
as I can think of, and

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.790
Donald says, Aaron,
that's not quite what we're looking for.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.830
How bout make it cuter?

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.170
Okay.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:22.740
Cute I can do.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:29.815
I put my hands behind my back,
I tap my foot and I stick out my butt.

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:34.240
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because, that's what cute does.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.655
And no surprise Donald says,
Aaron that is not a good look for you.

00:02:39.655 --> 00:02:45.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Go pelvic floor sexy.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.050
Pelvic floor sexy, okay maybe I can
do that, I swing out my leg and

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.490
I turn it around and I jump back and
I thrust forward my pelvis.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.840
And Donald says, better.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.800
This is the illustrative of
an eight year long journey

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.790
through tangents and metaphors.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.460
Stark visuals.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:07.006
Like go pelvic floor sexy.

00:03:07.006 --> 00:03:08.666
Move through molasses.

00:03:08.666 --> 00:03:11.310
Listen to the rope of your arms.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.770
Cut the air as a feather.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.050
All designs to bring texture, to movement.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.920
Now I loved my life.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.820
And I wasn't thinking about exits.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:25.750
I was lost in the content of
the words that I was experiencing.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.665
It's no surprise that if you look up
there, pelvis features prominently.

00:03:29.665 --> 00:03:32.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.300
I wasn't thinking about exits
because I thought I had time.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:40.836
I knew like all athletes, and others, my
career was going to end between 30 and 35.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.320
But as I was thinking I had time,
it turns out I was wrong.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.620
So for this next part of the story
let me put you in my shoes.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.660
It's two days before your 25th birthday.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.960
You wake up, and
you kiss the person sitting next to you.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.630
You pull on blue jeans that you bought
at Good Will three weeks ago and

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.390
you walk out into a foggy
San Francisco day.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:14.650
You get on a bus, you get off the bus, and

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:19.220
at work you exchange those blue jeans, or

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:24.780
blue tights, and you put on canvas ballet
slippers and you tie them tight so

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.400
every crevice of that canvas
matches every curve of your foot,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.430
and you put your leg up on that bar and
you stretch, and

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.750
you watch as the sun burns off the clouds
into what is turning into a beautiful day.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.150
Your boss comes up behind you.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.614
His name is Michael Smuin,
and he has shock white hair.

00:04:44.614 --> 00:04:48.010
He's put digital dance moves on Yoda,
he's choreographed for

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.100
Francis Ford Coppola and
he says to you, next year,

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.720
I want to choreograph a ballet for
you, Cyrano, long nose and all.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.602
What a great day.

00:05:01.602 --> 00:05:07.407
In the middle of class he says let's
have a jig, petit allegro, small jumps.

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:11.437
And in the middle of the studio you can
feel the glue from those canvas shoes

00:05:11.437 --> 00:05:15.272
cover your toes, and you walk to
the side expecting a correction, but

00:05:15.272 --> 00:05:18.998
when you look back at the center at
Michael something is very wrong.

00:05:18.998 --> 00:05:27.540
Michael isn't breathing.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.340
Your the first one at his side, and
you put an arm over his shoulder, and

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.109
you say loudly and
clearly Michael stay with us.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.640
Because you know hearing
is the last thing to go.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.810
You pick him up, and
you put him on the floor, and

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.745
you watch as his eyes roll
up into his head and close.

00:05:49.745 --> 00:05:54.866
Michael's death, Michael's

00:05:54.866 --> 00:05:59.610
exit, it is poetic for many.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:05.810
This man died doing what he loved.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.660
But, for me, it was a wake up call.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.330
It's hard to make a joke
at this point in the story.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.270
But if you look up at this list.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.600
Pelvis on a resume?

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:28.030
So like many others,
I went to community college.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.760
Monday to Wednesday I was in night school.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.410
Thursday to Sunday I was dancing,
and I was lucky to find myself

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.970
here at Stanford University as
an undergraduate at the age of 28.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:45.870
At the age of 28, I was watching a TED
talk as part of curriculum in a classroom.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.980
Let me say that again so
that you understand.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.690
I was watching a TED talk as
an assignment for a class.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:59.550
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sir Ken Robinson was

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:03.330
speaking about the value of creativity,
specifically divergent thinking.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:08.590
He was talking about divergent thinking
as the process of creating value

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:09.460
from an idea.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:14.670
And a small light when off in my head,
and it got brighter and

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.690
brighter as brighter as I was watching
this laptop, and I thought to myself,

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:21.970
oh my God, my entire dance career,

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:27.700
I was translating ideas into motion,
and that was the value of my life.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.720
I am a divergent thinker.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:33.760
That label had power.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:39.870
Here at the GSB,
as we get ready the students to leave,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.500
I ask, what are the labels that we have?

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.600
What are the processes that
we do that are different and

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.900
could change our lives as
we go out of this place?

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.580
And for those of you who are here
today who are not students.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.090
What has just occurred in this room?

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:57.760
What do we take when we exit?

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:05.363
Now when I ask that question I can't
help but think of a few things.

00:08:05.363 --> 00:08:09.938
&gt;&gt; I think put my arms in my legs.

00:08:09.938 --> 00:08:14.431
&gt;&gt; I think through tangents and metaphors.

00:08:14.431 --> 00:08:21.348
&gt;&gt; And I ask,
what do you take when you exit?

00:08:21.348 --> 00:08:22.510
Thanks.

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:29.441
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:29.441 --> 00:08:34.341
[MUSIC]

